⟦2700⟧ {{smaller block|<sup>4</sup> There appears to be a fundamental inclination towards ideas of rebirth and reincarnation (see F. B. Jevons, ''Introd. to Study of Comp. Religion'', 1908, pp. 50 sqq., 59 sqq.); it would seem to be wrapped up in the feeling of the essential "one-ness" of the group (including its deity), and involves the belief that such corporate bodies never die (cf. even the Roman conception of the family, Maine, ''op. cit.'' 197 sqq.).}}

⟦2900⟧ {{smaller block|<sup>5</sup> W. W. Fowler, ''Roman Festivals'', 103-105; Harrison, ''Journ. Hell. Stud.'' xix. 221. For the use of milk, cf. Frazer, ''Adonis'', 74 (with the suggestion that it is because milk is the food of babes), Crooke ii. 130, and F. Fawcett, ''Madras Gov. Bull.'' (1900), iii. 1, 58 (a South-Indian festival on the fifth of Srāvana, when the serpent-deity is ''bathed'' in milk).}}

